MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "The foam sheet and magnets fit my interest in making and adapting things: I can picture using them for protective pads, spacers, holders, and fixtures alongside my fasteners and adhesives.",
    "The vacuum chamber connects to my epoxy resin purchase through a possible next step in that work, provided it suits the resin. The pre-wired LEDs also appeal because they make small hands-on lighting experiments easier."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "Brad nails and deck joist tape require a compatible tool or a specific project that I have not established. Buying assorted fasteners and repair tape does not mean I currently need nailer supplies or decking materials.",
    "The MESTEK thermometer duplicates a recent purchase, and 800 identical wood screws offer less immediate value after my assortment purchases. The bathroom hooks and particular cleanroom swabs also lack a clear connection to a task I have underway."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "My interest in tools and repair supplies seems to have been stretched into an assumption that almost any home improvement item would be useful to me now.",
    "Some suggestions repeat the kind of product I already bought without giving me a clear additional use, especially the thermometer and bulk wood screws."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "I care about whether an item adds a useful capability to my existing supplies, such as cushioning materials or a possible aid for resin work, rather than merely resembling something I own.",
    "My repeated assortment purchases suggest that having sizes available for different tasks matters to me. A large quantity of one size, a tool-dependent fastener, or a specialized cleaning supply needs a more specific reason to buy."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The recommendations understood my hands-on making and repair interests, but sometimes missed the difference between a useful complement, a duplicate, and supplies for a project I have not started."
}
